course: dictionary
lesson: 13971
title: Literary words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Literary is a word about books. It means to do with literature: books, poems, plays and stories, and the people who write them. «Our class went to a literary festival where authors read their stories.» A literary festival is a festival about books, so the word tells you it belongs to the world of writing.
explain: Literary also describes a special kind of language. Writers choose fancy, colourful words to paint a picture in the reader's mind. «The moon hung like a silver coin above the sea» is literary language. In everyday talk you would just say, «The moon was bright.»
explain: To check a word like literary, ask: is this about books, poems, plays or stories? If yes, literary fits. Look for the clue words around it, such as «a literary prize» or «literary characters», and see whether they point to writing and reading.
fact: Literary means to do with literature: books, poems, plays and stories.
fact: Literary language is the special, fancy kind used in books, not in everyday talk.
fact: Literary devices are tricks writers use, such as simile and metaphor.
fact: A literary prize is an award given for writing.
fact: A good check is to ask: is this about books, poems, plays or stories?
exercise: «The class visited a literary festival where authors read their stories.» What does literary mean here? | To do with sport and games | To do with maths and numbers | *To do with books and writing | To do with cooking
exercise: «Her poem won a literary prize.» What kind of prize is it? | A prize for drawing a horse | A prize for running fast | A prize for cooking a meal | *A prize for writing
exercise: Which sentence uses literary language? | *The moon hung like a silver coin above the sea. | It is cold, so put on a coat. | The bus leaves at nine. | Pass me the salt, please.
test: Which word tells you something belongs to the world of books? | Muddy | Loud | Sunny | *Literary
test: What is literary language like? | Like everyday talk | Like a sign on a shop door | Like a shout in the playground | *Special and fancy, as in books
exam: Maya says «literary characters» are people who fix cars. Is she right? | *No, literary characters are people in books and stories | Yes, they are the workers at a garage | Yes, they fix cars in every town | No, they are people who sell food in shops
